; Regression: arithmetic on a REFERENCE must be diagnosed, not silently
; miscompiled (2026-08-23, the `ref[T]` consolidation, step 2).
;
; `&x` yields ref[T] since this change -- a borrowed reference is what the
; address-of operator MEANS, and pointer[T] is the implementor's escape hatch.
; But a ref carries no element stride: the pointer-arithmetic fallback in
; collapse_operand_sequence# keys on name = "pointer", so a ref LHS fell
; through with a fresh type var and emit_intrinsic_binary_op's GEP used BYTE
; stride. Measured on a two-field struct: `&b + 1` stepped 1 byte where the
; struct is 16, with rc 0 and no diagnostic anywhere.
;
; That is why the diagnostic had to land in the SAME commit as `&`-yields-ref:
; between the two halves the whole tree would compile silently wrong at every
; site that does pointer arithmetic on an address-of result.
;
; The deliberate exit is a cast -- see ref_address_of.scaly, which proves the
; stride comes back as 16.
;
; xfail: arithmetic on a reference

define Box (v: int  w: int)

var b Box(1, 2)
let p &b
let q p + 1
Console.println "unreachable"
